WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 171: Calvin Coolidge: The Roaring Twenties, 'Silent Cal's' Wit, and the Price of Duty (1924–1929)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.280
[Rebecca] Today we journey into the heart of the jazz age with the president who presided over its dizzying heights, Calvin Coolidge.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:21.920
[Rebecca] While flappers danced the Charleston and Wall Street sword, Silent Cal championed balanced budgets, tax cuts, and radical personal restraint.

5
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:30.320
[Rebecca] Yet behind the laconic humor lay a father's enduring grief and a leader who walked away from supreme power at the height of his popularity.

6
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:38.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the roaring twenties, the wit of Silent Cal, and the tragedy of the South Lawn.

7
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:47.280
[George] Between 1923 and 1929, the United States underwent the most rapid material expansion in human history.

8
00:00:47.280 --> 00:01:03.360
[George] Henry Ford's assembly lines turned out millions of affordable Model T automobiles, commercial radio stations broadcast into 9 million living rooms, motion pictures acquired sound, and electricity illuminated city skyscrapers and suburban homes.

9
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:10.000
[George] At the center of this whirlwind stood Calvin Coolidge, whose economic philosophy was disarmingly simple.

10
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:24.400
[George] Cut government spending to the bone, slash federal taxes to stimulate investment, retire the crushing national debt from World War I, and let American industry and individual initiative flourish without bureaucratic interference.

11
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.600
[George] Partnering closely with his brilliant, austere Treasury Secretary, Andrew W.

12
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:46.720
[George] Mellon, Coolidge reduced the top individual income tax rate from 58% down to 25%, eliminated income taxes entirely for lower-income Americans, and simultaneously ran massive federal budget surpluses every single year of his presidency.

13
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] Over his five and a half years in office, Coolidge paid down more than one-fourth of the entire national debt, leaving the federal government leaner, detroduced, and financially rock solid.

14
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:06.600
[George] To a booming nation, the calm in the White House was infectious, and Coolidge prosperity became the envy of the industrialized world.

15
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:15.880
[George] The dramatic expansion of consumer credit, electrification, and modern communications forged a truly unified national culture for the first time.

16
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:23.720
[George] Popular myth often portrays Calvin Coolidge as an aloof, backward looking recluse who refused to engage with modern media.

17
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:31.960
[George] In historical reality, Coolidge was the most technologically innovative president of his generation, the very first radio president.

18
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:45.640
[George] Where Theodore Roosevelt and Woodrow Wilson possessed booming, oratorical voices designed to project across crowded amphitheaters, Coolidge understood instinctively that the microphone was an intimate instrument meant for the family parlor.

19
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:56.600
[George] His dry, nasal Vermont cadence, spoken with crisp, unhurried enunciation, carried with remarkable clarity over the crackling static of early vacuum tube radios.

20
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:11.800
[George] When he delivered his 1925 inaugural address, an estimated 20 million Americans listened simultaneously across a coast-to-coast network of 21 stations, the largest collective audience assembled in world history up to that hour.

21
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:30.600
[George] Furthermore, far from hiding from journalists, Coolidge held an astonishing 520 press conferences during his presidency, an average of nearly two per week, routinely answering questions off the record with incisive clarity, mutual respect, and completely unvarnished candor.

22
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:35.880
[George] Coolidge understood that the American people did not need theatrical showmanship from their leader.

23
00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.280
[George] They desired honesty, clarity, and dependable consistency.

24
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:50.480
[George] By treating the press with professional candor and speaking directly to citizens in their homes, he mastered modern public relations with quiet, enduring brilliance.

25
00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:59.600
[George] Coolidge recognized that radio allowed a president to speak directly into the homes of ordinary families without journalistic spin or editorial filtering.

26
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:11.280
[George] Coolidge's legendary economy of words earned him the enduring moniker, Silent Cal, inspiring an entire folklore of dinner table anecdotes that he actively, gleefully cultivated.

27
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:18.560
[George] At a glamorous Washington Society dinner, a vivacious woman seated beside him leaned over and whispered, Mr.

28
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.920
[George] President, I made a bet with my friends that I could get you to say more than two words to me tonight.

29
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.520
[George] Coolidge paused.

30
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:31.120
[George] Looked directly into her eyes without the flicker of a smile, and replied, You lose.

31
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:39.200
[George] On another occasion, returning home from Sunday church services without Grace, his wife asked him what the minister had preached about.

32
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.280
[George] Sin, Coolidge replied.

33
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.400
[George] When Grace pressed him on what the minister had said about it, Coolidge replied simply.

34
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.840
[George] He was against it.

35
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.760
[George] He took quiet pleasure in befuddling his secret service guards.

36
00:04:51.760 --> 00:05:00.480
[George] Occasionally ringing the emergency buzzers in the Oval Office just to hide behind the window draperies and watch the frantic agents dash into the room.

37
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:11.600
[George] When asked by an interviewer why he attended so many tedious Washington dinner parties where he rarely spoke, Coolidge smiled faintly and replied, A man has to eat somewhere.

38
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:21.840
[George] His silence was never cold arrogance, it was a deliberate, protective armor against the pompous flatterers and self-serving lobbyists who constantly besieged the White House.

39
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.400
[George] His famous wit was never malicious.

40
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:30.000
[George] It was a gentle, razor-sharp needle puncturing the inflated vanity of the nation's political elite.

41
00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:40.920
[George] In the midst of political triumph and national adoration, tragedy struck the Coolidge family with devastating, heart-wrenching suddenness in the summer of 1924.

42
00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.200
[George] Coolidge's 16-year-old son, Calvin J.

43
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:56.920
[George] R., a handsome, athletic and gentle boy who shared his father's dry humor and unpretentious character, played a spirited game of lawn tennis on the south grounds of the White House with his older brother, John.

44
00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:03.800
[George] Playing in sneakers without socks, the boy developed a small friction blister on the third toe of his right foot.

45
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:11.000
[George] In an era before the discovery and mass production of penicillin, ordinary bacterial infections were deadly perils.

46
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:21.080
[George] Within forty-eight hours, the blister became violently infected with Staphylococcus aureus, spreading virulent blood poisoning through the young boy's bloodstream.

47
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:34.200
[George] For a week, while doctors desperately applied hot compresses and lanced the wound at Walter Reed Hospital, President Coolidge sat continuously by his son's bedside, weeping openly and holding his feverish hand.

48
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.560
[George] On July 7, 1924, Calvin J.

49
00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:37.880
[George] R.

50
00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.120
[George] died in his father's arms.

51
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:52.040
[George] The president would frequently walk alone to his son's empty bedroom, staring out the window in silent weeping, haunted by the feeling that had he remained in private life, his son would still be alive.

52
00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.600
[George] The blow shattered Coolidge's soul.

53
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.760
[George] As he later wrote with heartbreaking eloquence in his autobiography.

54
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] When he died, the power and the glory of the presidency went with him.

55
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.240
[George] I do not know why such a price was exacted for occupying the White House.

56
00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.680
[George] But the way of the world has been clear to me ever since.

57
00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:18.400
[George] The grief of losing his youngest son never left him, lending a quiet, haunting poignancy to his final years in the White House.

58
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:27.600
[George] Bearing the unbearable weight of his private bereavement, Coolidge entered the presidential campaign of 1924 under the slogan Keep Cool with Coolidge.

59
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:34.320
[George] The country, basking in low unemployment and stable prices, gave him an overwhelming vote of confidence.

60
00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:40.480
[George] Coolidge garnered 382 electoral votes, crushing both Democrat John W.

61
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.040
[George] Davis and Progressive Champion Robert La Follette by a decisive margin.

62
00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:55.600
[George] Throughout his presidency, Coolidge stood firmly on the side of racial and religious tolerance at a time when the second Ku Klux Klan held massive parades through the streets of Washington.

63
00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:08.640
[George] In June 1924, Coolidge proudly signed the Indian Citizenship Act, granting full United States citizenship and voting rights to all Native Americans born within the territorial boundaries of the country.

64
00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:18.640
[George] When a bigoted constituent wrote urging him not to allow an African American candidate to run for Congress on the Republican ticket, Coolidge wrote back a scorching rebuke.

65
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.000
[George] A colored man is just as much entitled to his rights under the Constitution as any other man.

66
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:31.280
[George] And I am not willing to believe that the day has come when a citizen can be denied his political rights because of his color or race.

67
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] Coolidge consistently urged Congress to pass federal anti-lynching legislation, condemning mob violence as an intolerable stain upon the American flag.

68
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:50.000
[George] He stood courageously against intolerance, proving that the ideals of American constitutional liberty must protect every citizen regardless of origin.

69
00:08:50.000 --> 00:09:02.760
[George] In June 1927, world history witnessed an explosion of global joy when 25-year old Charles Lindbergh completed the first solo nonstop transatlantic flight from New York to Paris in the spirit of St.

70
00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.400
[George] Louis.

71
00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:13.800
[George] Recognizing the immense cultural and diplomatic significance of the achievement, Coolidge dispatched the cruiser USS Memphis to bring the young aviator home in triumph.

72
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:29.320
[George] Standing before an ecstatic crowd of over 300,000 people on the Washington Monument grounds, Coolidge pinned the distinguished Flying Cross onto Lindbergh's lapel, delivering a moving, poetic oration celebrating American ingenuity and modest courage.

73
00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:35.080
[George] In foreign policy, Coolidge pursued a path of peaceful commerce and quiet diplomacy.

74
00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:47.480
[George] He refused to entangle the United States in European military alliances while successfully resolving contentious oil and property disputes with Mexico through his gifted ambassador, Dwight Morrow.

75
00:09:47.480 --> 00:10:03.480
[George] In 1928, his administration championed the Kellogg Bryant Pact, in which 62 nations solemnly pledged to renounce war as an instrument of national policy, an idealistic treaty reflecting Coolidge's genuine longing for enduring international tranquility.

76
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:11.880
[George] His calm statesmanship during a decade of rapid cultural upheaval provided the steady anchor that allowed American innovation to thrive.

77
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:21.480
[George] On August 2, 1927, the exact four-year anniversary of his midnight swearing in at Plymouth Notch, Coolidge stunned the political world.

78
00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:27.800
[George] Vacationing in the Black Hills of South Dakota, he summoned 30 reporters into a local schoolhouse.

79
00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:34.600
[George] Handing each reporter a small, two inch slip of paper with a single typed sentence, he said nothing.

80
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:39.800
[George] The slips read, I do not choose to run for president in 1928.

81
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:48.320
[George] Party leaders, industrialists, and ordinary citizens pleaded with him to reconsider, assuring him of an effortless, universal re-election.

82
00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.560
[George] Coolidge adamantly refused.

83
00:10:50.560 --> 00:11:02.160
[George] He believed ten years in the White House was too long for any man in a free republic, and he privately confided that he sensed economic troubles ahead that would require younger, more vigorous leadership.

84
00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:13.360
[George] In stepping down voluntarily when absolute power was his for the taking, Coolidge joined George Washington in upholding the supreme Republican ideal that no man should be indispensable.

85
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:28.320
[George] He retired quietly with Grace to the beaches, a peaceful estate in Northampton, Massachusetts, spending his final years writing his memoirs, walking his dogs, and warning against the speculative stock frenzy that was gripping the country.

86
00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:36.640
[George] On January 5, 1933, at the age of 60, Calvin Coolidge died suddenly of coronary thrombosis.

87
00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:45.760
[George] He remains an enduring symbol of unflinching honesty, humble public service, and the timeless truth that character is the true currency of statesmanship.

88
00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:54.560
[George] In walking away from supreme power at the absolute height of his fame, Calvin Coolidge affirmed that the office belongs to the people alone.

89
00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:00.240
[Rebecca] Calvin Coolidge departed the White House at the pinnacle of national peace and prosperity.

90
00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:11.520
[Rebecca] Having governed with a quiet dignity that modern politics rarely sees, but the roaring economic engine he left behind was about to encounter a catastrophic reckoning.

91
00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:16.400
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the tragic presidency of his successor, Herbert Hoover.

92
00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:29.280
[Rebecca] The Iowa orphan, the mining prodigy, the celebrated great humanitarian who fed millions of starving European children, and the crisis manager who was blindsided by the Wall Street crash of 1929.

93
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:34.760
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

